WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:06.910 --> 00:00:10.870
SPEAKER: Judaism embraces the intricate, religious, and cultural development

00:00:10.870 --> 00:00:15.820
of the Jewish people through approximately 30 centuries of history,

00:00:15.820 --> 00:00:18.890
stretching from the Bible to the Roman Empire

00:00:18.890 --> 00:00:22.480
to medieval Spain to the Enlightenment to modern times

00:00:22.480 --> 00:00:24.420
and the founding of the state of Israel.

00:00:24.420 --> 00:00:28.380
The result is an experience that reflects a mysterious relationship

00:00:28.380 --> 00:00:30.730
between religion and peoplehood.

00:00:30.730 --> 00:00:34.846
From a religious perspective, Judaism is a theistic system.

00:00:34.846 --> 00:00:36.720
But from a peoplehood perspective, it is also

00:00:36.720 --> 00:00:39.620
the group memory of the manifold communities

00:00:39.620 --> 00:00:42.610
and cultures formed through the ages.

00:00:42.610 --> 00:00:46.510
It consists not only of Torah, divine revelation,

00:00:46.510 --> 00:00:52.190
and mitzvot, divine commandments, but also diverse cultures and languages.

00:00:52.190 --> 00:00:58.960
Among others, Hebrew, Aramaic, Greek, Arabic, Yiddish, Ladino, and English.

00:00:58.960 --> 00:01:03.560
It includes politics, whether in Poland, America, or Israel.

00:01:03.560 --> 00:01:08.320
And it includes the whole range of Jewish education and family life, food,

00:01:08.320 --> 00:01:12.870
and festival, music and dance, and custom and humor.

00:01:12.870 --> 00:01:14.570
Judaism is not just a religion.

00:01:14.570 --> 00:01:19.600
It is the sum total of all the diverse elements that make Jews distinctive.

00:01:19.600 --> 00:01:23.240
Judaism as a religion is often summarized under three

00:01:23.240 --> 00:01:26.770
heads-- God, Torah, and Israel.

00:01:26.770 --> 00:01:30.380
Each of these elements has assumed varying positions of prominence

00:01:30.380 --> 00:01:31.800
throughout Jewish history.

00:01:31.800 --> 00:01:34.540
And an understanding of them necessarily unfolds

00:01:34.540 --> 00:01:38.080
within an ongoing historical and cultural framework.

00:01:38.080 --> 00:01:42.660
It was between 1,200 and 500 BCE that the Israelite people

00:01:42.660 --> 00:01:47.140
of the ancient Near East began to articulate a radical new understanding

00:01:47.140 --> 00:01:47.830
of divinity.

00:01:47.830 --> 00:01:51.930
The belief in a universal, singular god is arguably one

00:01:51.930 --> 00:01:54.780
of the greatest religious innovations among the world's

00:01:54.780 --> 00:01:57.000
historic religious systems.

00:01:57.000 --> 00:01:59.650
Through the visions in the voices of prophets

00:01:59.650 --> 00:02:04.700
the god the Israelites conceived was all powerful, yet benevolent, just,

00:02:04.700 --> 00:02:05.810
yet merciful.

00:02:05.810 --> 00:02:08.889
Rejecting the anthropomorphic tendency of the time,

00:02:08.889 --> 00:02:13.560
the Hebrews did not represent God in any human form or earthly likeness,

00:02:13.560 --> 00:02:18.770
but as a universal moral god engaged in a lasting relationship with humankind.

00:02:18.770 --> 00:02:22.620
These themes initially developed through oral literature,

00:02:22.620 --> 00:02:26.220
evolved over time into the written record of the Hebrew scriptures.

00:02:26.220 --> 00:02:29.010
Meaning literally instruction or guide book,

00:02:29.010 --> 00:02:33.640
the Torah is the central text of Judaism, referring most specifically

00:02:33.640 --> 00:02:38.740
to the first five books of the Bible called the Pentateuch-- traditionally

00:02:38.740 --> 00:02:42.250
thought to be penned by the early Hebrew prophet Moses.

00:02:42.250 --> 00:02:47.290
More generally, however, Torah applies to all of the Jewish sacred literature,

00:02:47.290 --> 00:02:48.560
learning, and law.

00:02:48.560 --> 00:02:52.620
These written traditions and the social relationships derived from them

00:02:52.620 --> 00:02:57.820
would continue to unfold in the context of shifting experiences of homeland,

00:02:57.820 --> 00:03:01.150
statehood, exile, and diaspora.

00:03:01.150 --> 00:03:04.840
The Jewish bookshelf contains many books beyond the Torah.

00:03:04.840 --> 00:03:09.490
The Hebrew Bible-- called by Jews the Tanakh and called by Christians

00:03:09.490 --> 00:03:12.700
the Old Testament-- includes the Torah to be sure

00:03:12.700 --> 00:03:17.320
but contains many books besides-- notably, the books of the prophets,

00:03:17.320 --> 00:03:19.620
the book of Psalms, and the Book of Job.

00:03:19.620 --> 00:03:22.250
Beyond the Bible, the Jewish book shelf contains

00:03:22.250 --> 00:03:27.590
books of law, ritual, scriptural interpretation, homilies, philosophy,

00:03:27.590 --> 00:03:33.200
history, poetry and much else composed from ancient to modern times.

00:03:33.200 --> 00:03:36.540
Among these works perhaps the best known is the Talmud--

00:03:36.540 --> 00:03:40.080
a massive work composed in Babylonia-- modern day Iraq--

00:03:40.080 --> 00:03:42.030
in the first centuries of our era.

00:03:42.030 --> 00:03:44.750
In literary form, the Talmud is a commentary

00:03:44.750 --> 00:03:48.310
on earlier work-- the Mishnah-- and in turn became

00:03:48.310 --> 00:03:51.960
the focal point of innumerable commentaries written in the following

00:03:51.960 --> 00:03:53.350
centuries.

00:03:53.350 --> 00:03:56.570
For religious Jews, the Talmud is the foundational document

00:03:56.570 --> 00:03:58.340
of Jewish law to this day.

00:03:58.340 --> 00:04:03.050
From both traditional and historical perspectives, the bond of peoplehood

00:04:03.050 --> 00:04:06.300
has informed Jewish identity throughout the centuries.

00:04:06.300 --> 00:04:10.370
Conversion to Judaism is possible, but the concept of family heritage

00:04:10.370 --> 00:04:11.950
remains paramount.

00:04:11.950 --> 00:04:16.370
The convert is welcomed as a new, adopted member of the family.

00:04:16.370 --> 00:04:19.220
In political terms, Jewish statehood found expression

00:04:19.220 --> 00:04:22.860
in early unification of the Israelite monarchy under King David.

00:04:22.860 --> 00:04:25.030
Though beginning with the exile in Babylonia,

00:04:25.030 --> 00:04:28.460
this was followed by much longer periods of dispersion

00:04:28.460 --> 00:04:33.750
to a varied set of global, cultural, social, and political contexts.

00:04:33.750 --> 00:04:36.720
Living apart in diverse regions over many centuries,

00:04:36.720 --> 00:04:39.810
Jews have taken on a multiplicity of cultural and even

00:04:39.810 --> 00:04:41.200
racial characteristics.

00:04:41.200 --> 00:04:44.180
But nonetheless, distinct elements of Jewish identity

00:04:44.180 --> 00:04:47.050
have persisted throughout history of diaspora.

00:04:47.050 --> 00:04:50.080
Theological and ethical thought within Judaism

00:04:50.080 --> 00:04:52.740
also continues to respond to the changing

00:04:52.740 --> 00:04:56.370
historical context through which Jewish communities have journeyed.

00:04:56.370 --> 00:05:00.360
Philo of first century Alexandria, [INAUDIBLE] Sa'adya Gaon

00:05:00.360 --> 00:05:05.980
of 10th century Babylonia, Maimonides of 12th century Spain and Egypt.

00:05:05.980 --> 00:05:08.670
Many great Jewish thinkers have taken pains

00:05:08.670 --> 00:05:11.680
to integrate the Judaism of the Torah and the Talmud

00:05:11.680 --> 00:05:14.540
with the best of contemporary thought.

00:05:14.540 --> 00:05:19.130
Modern times have seen the birth of secular Judaism, of Jewishness

00:05:19.130 --> 00:05:24.340
as ethnicity, peoplehood and culture shorn of religion.

00:05:24.340 --> 00:05:29.460
The most famous Jews of modern times-- Karl Marx, Sigmund Freud, and Albert

00:05:29.460 --> 00:05:34.680
Einstein, not to mention Woody Allen, Mel Brooks, and J. Robert Oppenheimer--

00:05:34.680 --> 00:05:37.860
all were or are secular Jews.

00:05:37.860 --> 00:05:42.630
Judaism remains vibrant in today's world as Jews understand and express

00:05:42.630 --> 00:05:46.950
their Jewishness in numerous, diverse ways.

